  :prose
"The vertical shoot-em-up is the arcade cabinet's most-copied form.
Player at the bottom. Enemies stream down. Bullets stream up. Sometimes
the enemies fire back. The screen fills with sprites; your thumb
becomes an instrument. In 1978 a game with fifty-five sprites broke
Japan's supply of the 100-yen coin the government had to mint more.

This chapter builds the whole shape in about a hundred lines. The heart
of it is a LAYER TABLE — a small data structure that lets us have four
kinds of entities on screen (player, enemy, player-bullet, enemy-bullet)
without bullets hitting their owner or enemies bumping other enemies. And
a per-kind list, which we walk to spawn a new wave without touching the
other kinds.

By chapter end you have a small shmup with waves, layered collisions,
and a difficulty that scales with the enemy count. Not a shipping game —
that is chapter 16 — but a full playable session in one page.

---

## 5.1 The player and the pool

Start with the player ship and a mechanism to fire bullets.

```scheme
;; An entity is (kind pos vel). The world is a list of entities.
(define (ent kind pos vel) (list kind pos vel))
(define (ent-kind e) (car e))
(define (ent-pos e)  (cadr e))
(define (ent-vel e)  (caddr e))

;; Move every entity by its velocity (one frame of motion).
(define (advance e)
  (ent (ent-kind e) (vec/add (ent-pos e) (ent-vel e)) (ent-vel e)))

;; The player is just an entity at the bottom. Left/right nudges its x;
;; firing conses a bullet moving straight up (vy = -6) into the world.
(define (move-player p dx)
  (ent 'player (vec/add (ent-pos p) (vec/make dx 0)) (ent-vel p)))

(define (fire-bullet p)
  (ent 'player-bullet
       (vec/make (car (ent-pos p)) (- (cadr (ent-pos p)) 10))
       (vec/make 0 -6)))

;; Cull bullets that have flown off the top of the screen.
(define (cull-bullets bs)
  (filter (lambda (b) (>= (cadr (ent-pos b)) 0)) bs))

;; Run 40 frames. The player fires every 10 frames; bullets rise and are
;; culled off the top. Report how many bullets are still on screen.
(define (player-only)
  (let ((p (ent 'player (vec/make 160 220) (vec/make 0 0))))
    (let loop ((f 0) (bs '()))
      (if (>= f 40)
          (length bs)
          (let ((bs2 (if (= 0 (modulo f 10)) (cons (fire-bullet p) bs) bs)))
            (loop (+ f 1) (cull-bullets (map advance bs2))))))))

(player-only)
;; => 3
```

The LAYER TABLE (built next, section 5.2) is an alist of layer-name to
allowed collision partners. A collision is only real if each side lists
the other. That is how bullets never collide with their spawner — we
just never put `player` in `player-bullet`'s partner list.

Note the bullet-cull at the bottom of the loop. Every bullet that has
flown off the top gets dropped by `filter`. Without this, the list
fills up with hundreds of dead bullets and it grows without bound.
Every arcade shooter does exactly this cleanup pass.

---

## 5.2 Add enemies

Enemies spawn at the top and drift down.

```scheme
;; The LAYER TABLE: which kinds may collide. Each row is
;; (kind . allowed-partners) written as a plain list. A collision is
;; real only if EACH side lists the other. player-bullet lists enemy
;; but not player, so a bullet can never hit its own ship.
(define layers
  '((player        enemy enemy-bullet)
    (enemy         player player-bullet)
    (player-bullet enemy)
    (enemy-bullet  player)))

(define (collides? ka kb)
  (let ((row (assq ka layers)))
    (and row (memq kb (cdr row)) #t)))

;; A hit = layers allow it AND the two are within 8px.
(define (hit? a b)
  (and (collides? (ent-kind a) (ent-kind b))
       (< (vec/norm (vec/sub (ent-pos a) (ent-pos b))) 8)))

;; Deterministic pseudo-random x from a seed (a cheap hash). Same seed,
;; same x — good for replay and for a stable book output. `random-int`
;; is available if you want true noise; see chapter 14 for tape replay.
(define (rand-x seed) (+ 20 (modulo (* seed 61) 280)))
(define (spawn-enemy seed)
  (ent 'enemy (vec/make (rand-x seed) -10) (vec/make 0 1.5)))

;; One frame: advance all, drop any bullet or enemy involved in a hit,
;; then cull the off-screen survivors.
(define (step-world bullets enemies)
  (let* ((bs (map advance bullets))
         (es (map advance enemies))
         (live-bs (filter (lambda (b)
                            (not (fold (lambda (e acc) (or acc (hit? b e))) #f es)))
                          bs))
         (live-es (filter (lambda (e)
                            (not (fold (lambda (b acc) (or acc (hit? b e))) #f bs)))
                          es))
         (bs2 (filter (lambda (b) (>= (cadr (ent-pos b)) 0)) live-bs))
         (es2 (filter (lambda (e) (<= (cadr (ent-pos e)) 250)) live-es)))
    (list bs2 es2)))

;; Run 60 frames. The player sits at x=140 — right under the column
;; where these enemies spawn — and fires every 8 frames. Every enemy
;; that drifts into a bullet dies. Report (bullets-alive enemies-alive).
(define (player-and-enemies)
  (let ((p (ent 'player (vec/make 140 220) (vec/make 0 0))))
    (let loop ((f 0) (bs '()) (es '()))
      (if (>= f 60)
          (list (length bs) (length es))
          (let* ((bs1 (if (= 0 (modulo f 8)) (cons (fire-bullet p) bs) bs))
                 (es1 (if (= 0 (modulo f 40)) (cons (spawn-enemy 80) es) es))
                 (stepped (step-world bs1 es1)))
            (loop (+ f 1) (car stepped) (cadr stepped)))))))

(player-and-enemies)
;; => (4 0)
```

We have a game. Ship moves. Bullets fire. Enemies stream in. Bullets
kill enemies. Enemies escape off the bottom. The layer table prevents
bullets from stopping on the player or ricocheting off other enemies —
`collides?` gates every pair before the distance test runs. Here every
enemy shares the shooter's column, so the four surviving bullets are the
ones still climbing after the enemies were shot down.

`rand-x` hashes a seed into an x in [20, 299] — deterministic, so the
book output is stable and a replay lands identically. Feed it the frame
number for a scatter, or swap in `(random-int 280)` when you want true
noise.

---

## 5.3 Enemies that shoot back

Add enemy fire. Enemies emit a bullet on a fixed pulse.

```scheme
;; An enemy fires a bullet straight down (vy = 3) from its position.
(define (enemy-shot e) (ent 'enemy-bullet (ent-pos e) (vec/make 0 3)))

;; Count the enemy-bullets within 8px of the player this frame — those
;; are the hits. `collides?` inside `hit?` gates the pair first.
(define (hits-on-player p ebs)
  (length (filter (lambda (b) (hit? b p)) ebs)))

;; Two enemies parked over the player's column fire down every 20
;; frames. We track hp (starts 3, clamped at 0 = dead) and an
;; accumulating CAMERA-SHAKE budget: each hit adds 4 to the shake the
;; renderer would spend that frame. Shake as a number, not a call.
(define (enemies-fire)
  (let ((p (ent 'player (vec/make 160 220) (vec/make 0 0)))
        (enemies (list (ent 'enemy (vec/make 160 40) (vec/make 0 0))
                       (ent 'enemy (vec/make 160 90) (vec/make 0 0)))))
    (let loop ((f 0) (ebs '()) (hp 3) (shake 0))
      (if (>= f 80)
          (list 'hp hp 'shake shake)
          (let* ((ebs1 (if (= 0 (modulo f 20))
                           (append (map enemy-shot enemies) ebs) ebs))
                 (moved (map advance ebs1))
                 (dmg  (hits-on-player p moved))
                 (hp2  (max 0 (- hp dmg)))
                 (surv (filter (lambda (b) (not (hit? b p))) moved))
                 (onscreen (filter (lambda (b) (<= (cadr (ent-pos b)) 240)) surv)))
            (loop (+ f 1) onscreen hp2
                  (if (> dmg 0) (+ shake 4) shake)))))))

(enemies-fire)
;; => (hp 0 shake 16)
```

Enemy fire is a periodic pulse: every 20 frames each enemy conses a
downward bullet into the world. We hook shot-firing by walking the
enemy list each frame rather than installing a per-enemy timer — for a
chapter that keeps its footprint small, a plain frame-gated pulse is
the honest primitive. A more elaborate treatment would give each enemy
its own fire-clock as a field.

The player's hp starts at 3 and drops by one per hit; `max` clamps it
at 0, where the ship is dead (the game-over trigger belongs to chapter
14; here we just track). The shake value is CAMERA-SHAKE AS DATA: each
hit adds to a number the renderer would read to jitter the view.
Chapter 6 turns that number into actual screen motion.

---

## 5.4 Entity pooling

At high enemy counts, spawning and despawning entities every few
frames can allocate memory that garbage collection has to reclaim. In
tight arcade action this matters. A pool is a fixed set of entities we
recycle: mark inactive, then reuse on next spawn.

```scheme
;; A pooled bullet is a SLOT: (alive? pos vel). Dead slots sit off-screen
;; at (-100 -100) waiting to be reused instead of freshly allocated.
(define (slot alive pos vel) (list alive pos vel))
(define (slot-alive? s) (car s))
(define (slot-pos s) (cadr s))
(define (slot-vel s) (caddr s))

;; Pre-build a pool of n dead slots.
(define (make-pool n)
  (map (lambda (i) (slot #f (vec/make -100 -100) (vec/make 0 0)))
       (iota n)))

;; Wake the first dead slot: give it a position + velocity. Returns the
;; whole pool with that one slot now alive. If none free, pool unchanged.
(define (spawn-from-pool pool pos vel)
  (let loop ((seen '()) (rest pool))
    (cond
      ((null? rest) (reverse seen))                 ;; exhausted
      ((slot-alive? (car rest))
       (loop (cons (car rest) seen) (cdr rest)))
      (else (append (reverse seen)
                    (cons (slot #t pos vel) (cdr rest)))))))

;; Retire a slot: send it back to the pen, mark dead.
(define (release-slot s)
  (slot #f (vec/make -100 -100) (vec/make 0 0)))

;; A 5-slot pool, wake three bullets, count how many are alive.
(define (pool-demo)
  (let* ((p0 (make-pool 5))
         (p1 (spawn-from-pool p0 (vec/make 10 10) (vec/make 0 -6)))
         (p2 (spawn-from-pool p1 (vec/make 20 20) (vec/make 0 -6)))
         (p3 (spawn-from-pool p2 (vec/make 30 30) (vec/make 0 -6))))
    (length (filter slot-alive? p3))))

(pool-demo)
;; => 3
```

This is not the only way. For most kid-scale games, allocating and
despawning is fine — the runtime is fast. But knowing the pool
pattern lets you SCALE. If you build a bullet-hell with 500 bullets on
screen and hit a frame-rate drop, come back here.

---

## 5.5 Waves and choreography

The next level of shmup design is coordinated enemy waves.

```scheme
;; A wave is DATA: a list of (frame x y) spawn events. `spawn-v-wave`
;; builds a V-formation of 8 enemies, one entering every 4 frames, the
;; wings staggered higher than the point of the V.
(define (spawn-v-wave center-x)
  (map (lambda (i)
         (list (* i 4)                          ;; when: frame
               (+ center-x (* (- i 4) 15))      ;; where: x
               (- -10 (* (abs (- i 4)) 10))))   ;; where: y (staggered)
       (iota 8)))

;; The full schedule for a center of 160.
(spawn-v-wave 160)
;; => ((0 100 -50) (4 115 -40) (8 130 -30) (12 145 -20) (16 160 -10) (20 175 -20) (24 190 -30) (28 205 -40))
```

The frame loop reads this list: on each frame it spawns every event
whose frame matches the counter, then drops it. Choreography as data —
the wave shape lives in the list of scheduled events, not in a nested
set of ifs. To fire a wave you just `append` its events onto the
pending schedule.

Real shmups build a whole library of waves this way — line, arc,
spiral, funnel — and pull one at random each round.

---

## 5.6 The four patterns in a shoot-em-up

**Composition.** Every entity in this chapter is composed from small
verbs: shape, layer, solid, hp, velocity, tag. Add or remove a verb
to change kind. The 'player' and 'enemy' entities differ only in
their layer and their solid/sensor flags.

**Orchestration.** The frame loop orchestrates: read input, spawn
enemies on the frame counter, spawn bullets on fire, advance every
entity, test the collision pairs and drop the losers by kind, cleanup
off-screen entities with `filter`, paint. Order matters HERE more than
in earlier chapters — if we tested collisions BEFORE advancing, we'd
resolve the previous frame's geometry.

**In-routing.** Three inputs (left, right, fire) route to three
player-state changes. The pair walk is routed by the (kind, kind)
pair — checked through the layer table — into different outcomes. That
routing table is the essence of the shmup's game logic.

**Routing.** Every event in the wave schedule routes a spawn to its
frame in the future. The schedule IS a router. Damage routes to hp;
a hit routes to the shake budget; an off-screen test routes to removal.
Almost every value the shmup produces routes through the loop to a
list.

---

## 5.7 Common desktop tasks

**Balloon-hunt promotion.** Enemies replaced by product-icons floating
across the top of a shop page. Shooter is a virtual arrow. Hit five
in ten seconds = free shipping. Layered collisions ensure you can't
double-count.

**Alert-triage minigame for the operator.** An operator has 30 pending
customer messages. Each message is an enemy drifting down; the
operator clicks the ones to auto-reply and lets the rest fall through
for manual handling. The visualization is a coping game for an
inbox-avalanche.

---

## 5.8 Pitfalls

**Bullets hit their own spawner.** Layer setup incomplete —
`player-bullet` still includes `player` in its collides-with list. Fix
the alist.

**Frame rate craters at 100 enemies.** You are collision-walking every
bullet against every enemy, which is O(n·m). For high counts use the
pool pattern and a spatial bucket so each bullet only tests nearby
enemies. Also: check that you are cleaning up dead entities (off-screen
bullets, etc.) each frame with `filter`.

**Enemy fires immediately on spawn, before moving on-screen.** Your
fire pulse fires while the enemy is still at y=-10. Gate firing with
`(when (>= (cadr (ent-pos e)) 0) ...)` so only on-screen enemies shoot.

**A collision is counted twice.** You tested `(hit? a b)` and
`(hit? b a)` and acted on both. Test each unordered pair once, or gate
by kind so only one direction resolves.

**Enemies stream in but never on-screen.** Your spawn y is above where
they can render, OR your enemy velocity is zero. Check the `vel`
component you pass to `ent` on spawn.

---

## 5.9 Try this yourself

1. Add a shield powerup: when the player has shield, damage decrements
   shield instead of hp. Shield decays over 10 seconds.
2. Add three enemy variants: fast-weak, slow-tough (2 hp), zigzag
   (velocity flips x every 20 frames).
3. Turn the shooter into an omni-shooter: 'a' fires down, 'b' fires
   up, 'up' fires up-left, 'down' fires up-right. Chapter 15 covers
   two-player; this is one-player two-thumbs.
4. Difficulty scaling: as `(length enemies)` grows over 20, shorten the
   spawn interval; when it drops, lengthen it. Self-balancing pace.
5. Boss preview: after 30 seconds, spawn one big enemy with 20 hp.
   Chapter 12 covers bosses; this is your first sighting.
6. Screen-clear bomb: 'b' sets `enemies` to `'()` and adds 20 to the
   shake budget. Use once per life.

---

## 5.10 What we built

A player, a bullet pool, waves of enemies, layered collisions, camera
shake on hit, hp tracking. All in about 100 lines. The core of a shmup
is that small.

Next chapter: the same physics, but we tune FEEL. Same ship — with
deadzone, camera lag, ease-in on rotation, screen-shake calibrated to
impact velocity. Chapter 6 is the polish pass.")

